{
  "id": "80674",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "Entropy",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "We can reason qualitatively about the tendency of a system to go in a direction that increases the dispersal of matter and energy. One of the most powerful tools we have is the ability to make this “tendency to disperse” quantitative through a quantity named "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "entropy"
              },
              {
                "text": " and symbolized as "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "S"
              },
              {
                "text": ". Entropy provides a precise, numerical value for the degree of dispersal in a system. You may have heard that "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "entropy ("
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "S"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ")"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A state function that describes the dispersal of matter and energy within a system, determined by the number of possible microstates; it quantifies the system's degree of randomness and distribution of energy."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "31810312",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "73126889",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " measures the degree of “disorder” of a system. This is because systems in which matter and energy are more dispersed generally appear to be more disordered. If you disperse your clothes around your bedroom instead of putting them neatly in drawers, your bedroom is more disordered. "
              }
            ],
            "id": "jqkcwaa7yjkgqok",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Entropy quantifies dispersal by considering the number of different arrangements the particles of the system can have. Let's consider ice, liquid water, and water vapor as examples. In the solid phase, the atoms or molecules are restricted to nearly fixed positions with respect to each other and are capable of only modest oscillations about these positions. In the liquid phase, the atoms or molecules are free to move over and around each other, though they remain in relatively close proximity to one another. This increased freedom of motion results in a greater variation in possible particle locations, so the number of ways in which the particles can be arranged has increased. Therefore, the liquid phase has greater entropy than the solid phase. Likewise, in the gas phase, the atoms or molecules occupy a much greater volume than in the liquid phase, so each atom or molecule can be found in many more locations than in the liquid (or solid) phase. Consequently, for any substance, "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "S"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "gas"
              },
              {
                "text": " > "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "S"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "liquid"
              },
              {
                "text": " > "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "S"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "solid"
              },
              {
                "text": "."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2nkn6nbgz4563mg",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "alt": "three flask the one on \n            the left is solid, it has a cube inside the flask and a magnification that shows many spheres \n            right next to each other in a cube shape. the flask in the middle is labeled liquid. inside the \n            flask is liquid with a defined volume. the magnification shows many spheres that are somewhat close \n            together but have a small amount of space between them. the fill the magnification area. the flask \n            on the right is labeled gas. inside the flask is a hazy, somewhat opaque green colored. the magnification \n            shows many particles that are siginificantly farther apart compared to the liquid.",
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "(a)"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " Solid: Highly ordered arrangement of particles. "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "(b)"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " Liquid: More possible arrangements of particles. Increase in entropy compared to solid. "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "(c)"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " Gas: Many more possible arrangements of particles. Increase in entropy compared to liquid. (By Yeya18 [Own work] [CC BY-SA 3.0 (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0)], via Wikimedia Commons.)"
                  }
                ],
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "rvew9it7trvnth5",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "a9zihbmy2cisyer",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/b5/b558b33fe2f60e5fb31966951a190d20/Mezclas_de_la_materia.jpg",
            "type": "img",
            "width": "600"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "If a chemical reaction or physical process results in a greater degree of disorder (entropy), the final entropy ("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "S"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "f"
              },
              {
                "text": ") is larger than the initial entropy ("
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "S"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "i"
              },
              {
                "text": "), and the value for Δ"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "S"
              },
              {
                "text": " is positive. If the process results in less disorder, "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "S"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "f"
              },
              {
                "text": " is smaller than "
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "S"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "i"
              },
              {
                "text": " and the value for Δ"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "text": "S"
              },
              {
                "text": " is negative."
              }
            ],
            "id": "s6qmey1b7t1d6w3",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "id": "4000059379",
        "selection": {
          "anchor": {
