      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "36mb4hh136zjttv",
                "text": "Entropy and Temperature"
              }
            ],
            "id": "xwt4pwf77a0p9nd",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "According to the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "kinetic molecular theory"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A model that describes the macroscopic behavior of gasses in terms of the microscopic motions of the atoms and molecules that comprise the gas."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1995403218",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1824332195",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ", the temperature of a substance is proportional to the average kinetic energy of its particles. Raising the temperature of a substance results in more extensive vibrations of the particles in solids and more rapid translations of the particles in liquids and gases. At higher temperatures, the distribution of kinetic energies among the atoms or molecules of the substance is also broader (more dispersed) than at lower temperatures. Therefore, the entropy for any substance increases with temperature."
              }
            ],
            "id": "42xhu2syd6cgh2i",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "alt": "Two graphs are shown. The y-axis of the left \n                    graph is labeled, “Fraction of molecules,” while the x-axis is labeled, “Velocity, v ( m / s ),” and has values of \n                    0 through 1,500 along the axis with increments of 500. Four lines are plotted on this graph. The first, labeled, \n                    “100 K,” peaks around 200 m / s while the second, labeled, “200 K,” peaks near 300 m / s and is slightly lower on \n                    the y-axis than the first. The third line, labeled, “500 K,” peaks around 550 m / s and is lower than the first two \n                    on the y-axis. The fourth line, labeled, “1000 K,” peaks around 750 m / s and is the lowest of the four on the \n                    y-axis. Each line get increasingly broad. The second graph has a y-axis labeled, “Entropy, S,” with an upward-facing \n                    arrow and an x-axis labeled, “Temperature ( K ),” and a right-facing arrow. The graph has three equally spaced \n                    columns in the background, labeled, “Solid,” “Liquid,” and, “Gas,” from left to right. A line extends slightly \n                    upward through the first column in a slight upward direction, then goes straight up in the transition between the \n                    first two columns. In then progresses in a slight upward direction through the second column, then goes up \n                    dramatically between the second and third columns, then continues in a slight upward direction once more. The first \n                    vertical region of this line is labeled, “Melting,” and the second is labeled, “Boiling.”",
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "When the temperature of a substance is raised, its particles have a higher average kinetic energy and a greater spread of kinetic energies, which corresponds to an increase in entropy. When a substance melts or vaporizes, it experiences a significant increase in entropy. The increase in entropy for vaporization is significantly larger than the increase in entropy for melting."
                  }
                ],
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "ibrfj54hdrgg74w",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "ikii2ztfk0qgwuy",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/75/754d75368e2caeba1f37323ed352e63d/CNX_Chem_16_02_EntGraph.jpg",
            "type": "img",
            "width": "700"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": ""
              }
            ],
            "id": "887307439",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "id": "1343115803",
