      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "ca7r5u876jg3tla",
                "text": "Determining the Sign of Δ"
              },
              {
                "em": true,
                "id": "ox9yvpnp2vvtbq2",
                "text": "S"
              },
              {
                "id": "3v3im3aloabuhec",
                "text": " for a Chemical Process"
              }
            ],
            "id": "gufpzv5ak0lkebs",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "We can apply the concepts of entropy to qualitatively determine the entropy change of a chemical process. First, let's consider an ionic solid, such as sodium chloride, dissolving in water: "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": " ",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "legacyBlockRendered": true,
                "src": "\\mathrm{NaCl}(s)\\;\\longrightarrow\\;\\mathrm{Na}^+(aq)\\;+\\;\\mathrm{Cl}^-(aq)",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula_inline"
              },
              {
                "text": "As a solid, the NaCl is confined to a much smaller volume and the ions are greatly restricted in terms of possible arrangements. When the compound is dissolved in water, the Na"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": " and Cl"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": " ions are free to move in the water, resulting in many more possible arrangements of particles and an increase in the volume occupied. Dissolving an ionic compound generally results in an increase in entropy. (Note: Not all dissolving processes have an increase in entropy. In some cases, the attractions between the ions in solution and the surrounding water molecules are strong enough that the number of possible arrangements of particles decreases.)"
              }
            ],
            "id": "d1y7g7gqwo3z1mo",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Next, let's consider the combustion of ethane gas:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "r9ulwnfznmfplxr",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "fu5dov73d5erpjx",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "c9hlhtri8kcvud1",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "2\\;{\\mathrm C}_2{\\mathrm H}_6(g)\\;+\\;7\\;{\\mathrm O}_2(g)\\;\\;\\longrightarrow\\;4\\;{\\mathrm{CO}}_2(g)\\; +\\;6\\;{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O(l)",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Note that there are nine reactant gas molecules (2 C"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "6"
              },
              {
                "text": " and 7 O"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": ") and only four product gas molecules (4 CO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "). When the number of gas molecules is increased as a result of a chemical reaction, there is an increase in entropy. If, as is the case in this example, there is a decrease in the number of gas molecules, there is a decrease in entropy."
              }
            ],
            "id": "nu8plwfj2b1x45z",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "id": "2469950042",
        "selection": {
          "anchor": {
            "offset": 0,
            "path": [
              0,
              0
            ]
          },
          "focus": {
            "offset": 0,
            "path": [
              1,
              0
            ]
          }
        },
        "type": "content"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Determine the sign of Δ"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "S"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " for the following process:"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "g9oyv6cewe1wq0m",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "id": "5zm5wzm1h460hwx",
                    "text": " ",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1y30iryx68lejkz",
                "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
                "src": "2\\;{\\mathrm{CO}}_2(g)\\;\\;\\longrightarrow\\;\\;2\\;\\mathrm{CO}(g)\\;+\\;{\\mathrm O}_2(g)",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "In this reaction, two moles of gas (2 CO"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ") produce three moles of gas (2 CO and 1 O"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "). Because the number of gas particles increases when reactants are converted to products, there are more ways to arrange the particles after the reaction occurs, and Δ"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "S"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " is positive. "
                  }
                ],
                "id": "abghmkejgv2mo8s",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Determine the sign of Δ"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "S"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " for the following process:"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "o6bc3fsg7csuu9g",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "id": "8avc5e6cet5cray",
                    "text": " ",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "k5xm6r7u47ax8nc",
                "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
                "src": "{\\mathrm H}_2{\\mathrm{CCH}}_2(aq)\\;+\\;{\\mathrm{Br}}_2(aq)\\;\\;\\longrightarrow\\;\\;{\\mathrm H}_2{\\mathrm {BrCCBrH}}_2(aq)",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "In this reaction, two moles of particles in solution (1 H"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "CCH"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " and 1 Br"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ") produce one mole of particles in solution (1 H"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "BrCCBrH"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "). Because there are fewer particles in solution after the conversion of reactants to products, the number of possible arrangements of the particles has been reduced. This corresponds to a decrease in entropy, so Δ"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "S"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " is negative."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "tuxhf19efx8u61g",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "id": "2501444779",
